#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Stream Zerodha (Kite) order updates over a websocket and merge them into the Redis hash `zerodha:orders:orders`, and
keep streaming until stopped.

    bin/zerodha/order_updates
    redis-cli HGETALL zerodha:orders:orders
    redis-cli HGET zerodha:orders:orders <order id>

Self-contained: it logs in with `ZerodhaAPI` and carries its own connection to Kite's ticker at
`wss://ws.kite.trade`, so it needs no other feed process or key.

**The stream.** Kite has no separate order endpoint: order updates arrive as JSON *text* messages on the same
ticker socket that carries market data as binary. This opens its own connection and subscribes to no
instruments, so the only traffic on it is order updates - a quotes reconnect can never drop an order event,
and no instrument quota is spent. It holds one of the three connections Kite allows per api_key, which is why
`bin/zerodha/quotes` uses at most two. The credentials travel in the URL, so there is no login message. Every
change to an order in the account arrives as `{"type": "order", "data": {...}}`, the data being Kite's order
postback: `order_id`, `exchange_order_id`, `parent_order_id`, `status`, `status_message`, `tradingsymbol`,
`exchange`, `instrument_token`, `transaction_type`, `order_type`, `product`, `validity`, `variety`,
`quantity`, `filled_quantity`, `pending_quantity`, `cancelled_quantity`, `price`, `trigger_price`,
`average_price`, `order_timestamp`, `exchange_timestamp`, `tag` and more.

**The key.** `zerodha:orders:orders` is the one hash for Zerodha's orders, keyed by `order_id` and shared with
`bin/zerodha/orders`, which merges the polled order book into it. Each update is written as
`{"observed_at": <epoch received>, "source": "websocket", "order": <normalized order>, "data": <data>}`, with Kite's
order exactly as sent in `data`. Kite's postback is the same object as an order-book row, so `order` is built by the
same function the poller uses - see `bin/zerodha/orders` for its fields. A websocket update always replaces its
order's entry; a polled row replaces it only when the entry was observed before the poll's request was sent. The hash
expires at 06:00 IST, moved to the next 06:00 by every write.

**The persistence stream.** Every update is also appended, in arrival order, to the Redis Stream
`zerodha:order-updates:stream` as an `update` field holding the same JSON, which `bin/zerodha/persist_orders`
writes to `zerodha.order_updates` - so the history the hash overwrites is kept there. Capped at about 20,000
entries.

**Positions.** Kite streams no position or holding updates, so there is no positions key. A text message of
any type other than `order` is logged, so one appearing would be noticed; binary frames are market data this
socket never subscribed to and are ignored.

**Sessions.** The token is read afresh on every connect, so a login made by any process is picked up. Kite
refuses a dead token at the handshake with HTTP 403; that, or a socket failing to open six times running, logs
in again through `ZerodhaAPI` before reconnecting - once, since Kite issues one token per session and every
login invalidates the last. A reconnect otherwise backs off from 1 to 60 seconds. Updates arrive only when an
order changes, so a quiet socket is not a broken one.

Exits 0 when stopped with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM, and 1 when a login fails or the socket is still refused after
logging in again. Reads only - it never places, modifies or cancels an order.
"""

import sys
import json
import signal
import threading
from datetime import datetime, timedelta
from pathlib import Path
from zoneinfo import ZoneInfo

